
Lane Detection
The Problem

A vehicle with a forward-facing camera has to work out three things from each frame it receives:

    Where the lane markings are.
    Where the centre of its lane is.
    Which lane it is in.

The arena gives you two markings: a dashed white centre line dividing the two lanes, and a solid yellow boundary line at the outer edge of the road.

Detecting the lane is not the end goal. The pipeline has to end in a value the vehicle can steer on - something that tells it how to correct its path, not just an image with the lane drawn over it.

The arena has turns

Ask of every method you consider: what does this return when the lane curves? Anything that can only describe a straight line will answer a curve with a chord through it. The other trap is that the centre line is dashed, so your detector cannot assume continuous evidence.
Approach 1 - Bird’s Eye View

The lane markings all lie on the flat ground plane, so a single homography relates the camera image to an overhead view of the road. Warp by its inverse and you get a top-down image - Inverse Perspective Mapping.
Step	Look up
Isolate the white and yellow markings by colour	HSV, cv2.inRange
Warp to a top-down view	cv2.getPerspectiveTransform, cv2.warpPerspective
Find which pixels belong to which line	column histogram, sliding window
Fit a curve through each line	np.polyfit, second order
Compute the offset and label the lane	Your own logic

Why it is worth the warp: parallel markings stay parallel, pixels convert to real distance by a constant scale, and a curved marking becomes a well-behaved function of the vertical axis which fits x=ay2+by+cx=ay2+by+c, not y=f(x)y=f(x).

The hard parts are choosing the four source points, which is a one-time calibration that every downstream number inherits, and keeping the dashed line’s patchy evidence from breaking the search.
Approach 2 - Working in the Camera View

Skip the warp and find the markings directly in the perspective image. Faster to get running, no calibration.

Mask a region of interest, run Canny, and pass the edges to a Hough line transform where every edge pixel votes for the lines through it, so a dashed line still accumulates one strong peak. cv2.HoughLinesP returns segments you can group by slope sign.

The catch is in the name: on a curve it returns fragmented chords. Either run Hough within horizontal strips and treat the road as piecewise-linear, or use it only to locate the markings and then fit a curve through the pixels.

Fitting a curve instead

Skip line detection altogether: reduce the mask to points - say the marking centroid in each horizontal band - and fit a polynomial or spline through them, with no straight-line assumption anywhere.

One stray cluster of bright pixels is enough to pull a least-squares fit off the marking. RANSAC discards such outliers instead of averaging them in.
Other Directions

Once the basics work, these are worth a look:

    Use the lane you found in the previous frame as the starting point for the next one, instead of searching the whole image again.
    Work out how sharply the road is turning from the curve you fitted, so the vehicle can start steering into the bend early.
    Deep learning models that label the lane pixels directly, which is how real self-driving systems do it.

